Date: 2012 December 16
Summary: Thoughts on the Ruby Design Process.

# The Future of Ruby

[Ruby](http://www.ruby-lang.org/) is a wonderfully object-oriented language with a Smalltalk heritage, combined with the meta-programming prowess and functional nature of Lisp. Grafted over these are [linguistic nuances][linguistics] derived from Perl. Matz has been working on Ruby for **20 years**, adding his own ideas and flavour to the language.

Ruby has attracted a sizeable following --- programmers who care about expressiveness, flexibility and aesthetics. Together we've built a vibrant ecosystem of tools, libraries and frameworks.

Like many, **I write Ruby for a living.** Every developer or organization heavily invested in Ruby should be interested in its future direction.

## Ruby Design Process

Over the past few years, Ruby implementations have sprung up everywhere. While [MRI][] continues to be the **reference implementation,** we also have [JRuby][], [Rubinius][], [MacRuby][], [RubyMotion][], [MagLev][], [IronRuby][], [mruby][], and most recently [Topaz][].

Ideally you should be able to run your Ruby code on any implementation. This raises an important question: **What is the definition of Ruby?** If MRI is the definition of Ruby, how can we tell what behaviours are intentional, rather than just [accidents of the implementation][whitespace]? If we, as Rubyists, rely on misfeatures of MRI, every implementation of Ruby has the burden of supporting those behaviours.

At RubyConf 2012, [Brian Ford](https://twitter.com/brixen) gave a talk entitled "[Toward a Design Process for Ruby][brixen_talk]". He discussed both technical and social challenges he has faced while implementing Rubinius, as well as challenges the Ruby community will face to remain relevant. I highly recommend listening to his talk.

On December 10th, the developers from various Ruby implementations held [a meeting on IRC][IRC] to discuss how the design process can be improved. That this meeting happened at all is a truly beautiful thing.

While no conclusions have been made, Brian [followed up with a blog post][brixen_blog] and [further discussion in Redmine][brixen_discuss].

In my opinion, Brian's plan is too far-reaching. Smaller steps are needed.

## Borrowing From Our Neighbours

I feel that looking to other language communities is the most appropriate starting point for improving Ruby's design process.

While I don't consider myself a Pythonista, I do have a lot of _respect_ for the Python community and their processes. 

Take a read through the [Python Enhancement Proposal: Purpose and Guidelines][pep1].

Here are the salient points:

* They have a BDFL, for Ruby **we have Matz.**
* Anyone can champion an enhancement proposal. It isn't restricted to a committee of core developers.
* Ideas are discussed on the mailing list first, to save the author(s) from drafting up a "static typing" proposal, only to have it rejected.
* Whether the proposal is accepted or rejected is up to the BDFL.
* A core developer may nominate him/herself as the "BDFL delegate" for a proposal. If the BDFL and core team agree, the delegate has final say over the proposal (eg. the concurrency model).
* The proposals are text documents in a version control system, as well as being published on the web site.

If you scan through the [list of proposals][pep0], you will find GvR next to 19 finalized proposals and 6 rejected proposals. This is a really wonderful resource. If I'm wondering *why Python doesn't have blocks*, I can go read [Guido's proposal](http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0340/) and why it was rejected.

Wouldn't it be nice to see Matz's proposal for *refinements*, and its subsequent acceptance -- or rejection. :-P

It's unlikely that Ruby's process would be exactly like Python's, but it's **a good starting point**. Maybe a draft could be submitted in either Japanese or English, with the editors providing a bilingual translation before it could be accepted? We would probably pick Git and Markdown, with many other details yet to be worked out.

Proposing enhancements to Ruby in this way would be a significant change/improvement to the current process.

## Language Reference

Having a process to propose enhancements to Ruby is great for the future of Ruby, but what about the present?

If someone were to say "Ruby is defined by its' implementation", we could not argue:

>"Not true -- what's generally considered the de facto reference implementation (CPython) has some aspects that are NOT part of the Python Language (as defined in the Reference), such as reference counting, the GIL…" - Alex Martelli on [Stack Overflow][so]

We need a comprehensive language reference. Is the [Ruby Core Reference][coreref] the closest thing we have to the [Python Language Reference][langref]? :-(

A publicly available document that the average Rubyist can and should read, so we *all* recognize the difference between what it _"Ruby proper"_ and what is an implementation detail. (eg. encodings on *any* object?).

It's not the same as RubySpec or the IPA/ISO spec. Implementors would want a formal specification like that. This is a reference for users of the language. 

Documenting Ruby as it exists today, and written enhancements proposals will be a lot of work. No question. But we gain the benefits of **document driven design**. If a feature is difficult or embarrassing to explain to your users, then maybe it isn't a good idea. :-)

Thank you for Ruby and its many implementations. Here's to hoping it only gets *better with age.*

